-- generation.separator_ids() -> every entity id that is a sequence separator rather than content.
--
-- ONE BODY FOR ONE TRUTH (§15). The category-name literals were previously typed
-- inline in corpus_whitespace_vocab_indices; a second call site copying them is the
-- divergence this law exists to prevent. Every caller resolves through here.
--
-- TWO TIERS, ONE RULE. A separator is an atom whose Unicode general category says
-- so, OR a grapheme cluster composed entirely of such atoms. The second arm is not
-- a special case: UAX#29 GB3 makes CR+LF a single grapheme, so the cluster is a
-- first-class entity at tier 1 and can never carry a category attestation of its
-- own -- categories are atom-level facts. Composition lifts the property (§3,
-- composition is Merkle), so bool_and over the realize.constituents IS the definition.
--
-- Measured on the foundation substrate 2026-07-29: 84 atoms + 1 grapheme (CRLF)
-- = 85 ids, 1.2 s for the whole set. Bounded by the alphabet, not by the corpus --
-- the same class of lookup as laplace.relation_type_id(), and explicitly NOT a corpus
-- cache. Nothing here scans ingested content.
--
-- NO RENDER FALLBACK. An earlier shape reached for realize.is_all_whitespace(render_text_
-- fast(...)) whenever a token had no category attestation. That was compensating
-- for the grapheme arm being missing, and it hid the reason: composites are
-- unattested BY CONSTRUCTION, not by incomplete seeding. Rendering to classify is
-- also the read-side law inverted -- classification is an indexed read on the id;
-- the realize.render is the cost.
--
-- SCRIPT HONESTY: Zs/Zl/Zp/Cc are the Unicode separator and control categories.
-- For a script that does not delimit with separator characters (Han, Thai,
-- Japanese) the set is simply empty for its atoms and the sequence is
-- content-contiguous -- the correct answer, not a silent failure.
DROP FUNCTION IF EXISTS laplace.separator_ids();
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION generation.separator_ids()
    RETURNS bytea[]
    LANGUAGE sql STABLE AS $$
    -- THE CLUSTER ARM IS A HASH, NOT A SEARCH.
    --
    -- A separator-only grapheme cluster is alphabet-bounded, so it does not have to
    -- be DISCOVERED. Entity identity is content-addressed: laplace.word_id(text) is a
    -- BLAKE3 hash of the CONTENT, at every tier -- laplace.word_id(chr(32)) IS the space
    -- atom's id, verified byte-for-byte. CR+LF is not a special case: it is a
    -- composition of two codepoints, hashed exactly as 'as' is, and composition lifts
    -- the separator property (§3, composition is Merkle -- the bool_and this body used
    -- to compute WAS that definition). UAX#29 GB3 is only what makes CR+LF the one
    -- such composition that is a single grapheme cluster, so it is the one the corpus
    -- carries: laplace.word_id(chr(13)||chr(10)) = cafeb486a01baa2c1ee30a8b78e2ff4d,
    -- verified equal to the single non-atom id the previous body returned.
    --
    -- The previous body found that one id by scanning EVERY tier-1 entity with a
    -- trajectory and testing bool_and over its constituents. Measured: atoms arm
    -- 58 ms, whole function 9,450 ms warm -- the entire cost was rediscovering a
    -- constant. Its own comment already claimed the set was "bounded by the alphabet,
    -- not by the corpus"; it was not, and now it is.
    --
    -- STATED DIFFERENCE: the hash is unconditional, so CRLF is returned whether or not
    -- that cluster has been ingested yet. Callers use this set for MEMBERSHIP
    -- (id = ANY(separator_ids())), where an id no entity carries simply never matches.
    -- Bounded by construction is the property the callers need.
    --
    -- Approaches measured and rejected: a first-constituent index pre-filter returns
    -- 599,753 rows (a leading separator is common); structural.containers_of over the 84
    -- atoms costs 25.7 s and truncates at its 1,000-row cap.
    WITH atoms AS (
        SELECT DISTINCT a.subject_id AS id
        FROM laplace.attestations a
        JOIN laplace.canonical_names cn ON cn.id = a.object_id
        WHERE a.type_id = laplace.relation_type_id('HAS_GENERAL_CATEGORY')
          AND cn.name IN ('unicode/category/Zs/v1',
                          'unicode/category/Zl/v1',
                          'unicode/category/Zp/v1',
                          'unicode/category/Cc/v1')
    )
    SELECT array_agg(id) FROM (
        SELECT id FROM atoms
        UNION
        SELECT laplace.word_id(chr(13) || chr(10))
    ) s
$$;
